change:
  id: CHG-022
  title: Give the reliability objective something to measure
  status: proposed
  intent: Let each behaviour case run more than once so reliability grades how consistently a result holds, instead of restating its own gate and sitting at 1.0 for every candidate that promotes.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - CON-002
    - PAT-004
    - ARCH-001
    - SYS-001

deltas:
  - op: MODIFIED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: Reliability measures consistency across repeated runs of a behaviour case, so two candidates that both clear every gate can score differently.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: a behaviour case is declared with repeated runs and one repeat fails
        then: reliability is the pass fraction across all runs and the gates are unaffected
        and:
          - a flaky candidate scores strictly lower than a consistent one, which is the discrimination among winners this exists to provide
      - id: S2
        when: a repeated run fails
        then: the deterministic-checks gate still passes
        and:
          - repeats grade rather than gate, or one flaky run would discard rather than lower a score, which is the trap that kept this objective pinned
      - id: S3
        when: a repeated run has produced evidence
        then: it stays in the recorded evidence
        and:
          - withholding narrows the gate only, never the record, so a lowered score can be traced to the run that lowered it
      - id: S4
        when: a caller declares one run per case
        then: reliability keeps its previous meaning including the timeout rule
        and:
          - a pass fraction at a single run would duplicate task_success, which is already the pass fraction of the same behaviour cases, and would double-weight failing a case rather than measure anything new
      - id: S5
        when: repeated runs are constructed
        then: each carries the same command as its canonical run and a distinct name
        and:
          - repeats are added after script resolution, so a repeat cannot introduce a script path that was not already required to exist and be executable
          - safety probes are not repeated, because they already grade and repeating them would change what their pass fraction means
      - id: S7
        when: enough repeats fail that the weighted sum falls below the promotion threshold
        then: the candidate is discarded although every gate passed
        and:
          - repeats grade rather than gate, but a graded objective still moves the sum and the threshold still applies, which is exactly how a failing safety probe behaves
      - id: S8
        when: the operator asks for repeats through the CLI
        then: each behaviour case is executed that many times and every result reaches the transcript under its own name
        and:
          - asserted end to end, because a unit test that injects repeat evidence into the scorer stays green even when nothing schedules a repeat
      - id: S9
        when: a run count is outside the supported range
        then: it is rejected
        and:
          - each repeat re-executes a candidate-authored script, so an unbounded count schedules work rather than producing evidence
      - id: S6
        when: a behaviour case is declared with a name ending in the repeated-run suffix
        then: it is rejected
        and:
          - such a name would collapse onto a different base name when runs are grouped, so two distinct cases would be scored as one

acceptance:
  - scenario: S1
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.aFlakyCandidateScoresLowerThanAConsistentOneAndBothStillPromote
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S2
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.aFailedRepeatRunDoesNotFailTheChecksGate
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S3
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.theFailingRepeatRunStaysInTheRecordedEvidence
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.asingleRunKeepsTheObjectiveUnchanged
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S5
    test_id: adapters/BehaviourCaseChecksTest.repeatedRunsShareTheCanonicalCommand
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S5
    test_id: adapters/BehaviourCaseChecksTest.probesAreNotRepeated
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S6
    test_id: adapters/BehaviourCaseChecksTest.aCaseMayNotBeNamedForARepeatedRun
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S7
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.aSufficientlyUnreliableCandidateFallsBelowTheThreshold
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S8
    test_id: cli/EvolveContractAcceptanceTest.reliabilityRunsExecuteEachBehaviourCaseAgain
    layer: acceptance
  - scenario: S9
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.anUnboundedRunCountIsRejected
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: deterministic/ReliabilityRunsTest.asingleRunStillDropsOnATimeout
    layer: unit

tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: add reliabilityRuns to ScoringConfig with a repeat-run naming convention and base-name collapsing
    status: completed
  - id: T2
    slice: score reliability as the pass fraction across runs, keeping single-run semantics unchanged
    status: completed
  - id: T3
    slice: withhold repeat runs from the deterministic-checks gate while keeping them in the evidence
    status: completed
  - id: T4
    slice: construct repeated runs after script resolution and expose --reliability-runs
    status: completed
  - id: T5
    slice: derive the withheld repeat names from the configuration rather than recognising a name pattern, so a check that merely looks like a repeat cannot escape the gate
    status: completed
  - id: T6
    slice: bound the run count, and assert the wiring end to end because the unit tests inject repeat evidence and cannot see a disconnected runner
    status: completed

non_goals:
  - task_success, which is pinned at 1.0 among promoted candidates for the same structural reason and needs a broader evidence source than the gated cases
  - renormalising the weighted sum over only the objectives a run actually measured, which is a separate correction to undeclared objectives scoring 1.0
  - population and ranking, which this unblocks but does not implement

risks:
  - repeated runs multiply wall-clock cost by the run count, so a caller trades time for signal. The default of one run keeps existing runs unchanged
  - a case that fails every run lowers both task_success and reliability, so an outright failure is weighted twice. The marginal content of reliability is consistency among cases that do pass, which is the case that matters for choosing parents
  - a behaviour case named with a .run<digits> suffix would collapse onto a different base name when runs are grouped, so declaring one is now rejected outright rather than silently scored as another case
